---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/use-cases/email-design';
const title = "Design a recovery email that survives missing product data";
const desc = "The design job is to make the shopper\u2019s next action understandable across real content states. A polished static template is only the first case to inspect.";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a href="/use-cases" class="link text-sm font-bold">← All use cases</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-slate-700">{desc}</p>
    <div class="prose-shopify mt-12">
      <h2>Prioritize the recovery action</h2>
      <p>Lead with a recognizable product or checkout context and a descriptive return link. Keep important price, offer terms and action text readable without images. Use meaningful image alternatives and link labels; do not put the only expiry or eligibility explanation inside a banner.</p>
      <h2>Design failure states</h2>
      <p>Specify fallbacks for missing images, very long product names, multiple variants, empty personalization and unavailable items. Hide broken blocks rather than printing raw placeholders. Distinguish the current catalog price from the offer actually available to this shopper, especially when a sale changes after the cart was created.</p>
      <h2>Protect the destination</h2>
      <p>Check that the primary action returns to the intended store, market and supported cart state. Do not expose customer details in visible URLs or forwardable content unnecessarily. A shared or expired link needs a safe fallback, with a clear route to rebuild the basket or ask for help.</p>
      <h2>Proposed rendering matrix</h2>
      <p>Preview representative messages on narrow screens, with images disabled, dark appearance and keyboard navigation, then inspect the clients your audience actually uses. Include long translations and currency formats. These are proposed checks, not tests performed for this guide; record the template version and actual failures when your team runs them.</p>
      <h2>Choose an editor by maintainability</h2>
      <p>Ask a candidate supplier to edit a reusable product block, show its fallback and export the resulting message for inspection. Sequenzy is a possible candidate only if it demonstrates the required catalog and rendering behavior. Evaluate who can safely update the template when merchandising changes, rather than selecting a tool from an unsupported design ranking.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
